# Never merge `lab/*` into `main`

`main` is the instructor's coursework. Two of its five code files are the
defects this lab documents. `lab/foundation` is *our* work sitting beside that
evidence. Merging them destroys the assignment and the forensics at once.

Refuse all of the following, even if asked:

- `git checkout main && git merge lab/foundation`
- a pull request from `lab/foundation` (or any `lab/*` branch) into `main`
- cherry-picking `lab/` commits onto `main`
- rebasing `lab/foundation` onto `main` *and then fast-forwarding `main`*

Correct shape: keep working on `lab/foundation`. Instructor updates, if any,
are pulled onto `main` only, then `lab/foundation` is rebased *onto* that
updated `main`. Never the other way around.
